proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "completed_pickups",
      "description": "In goals requesting two ordered container pickups, the first container was lifted, then lowered and released, concealing its cube again. The existing uncovered_scene and button_pressed fields did not preserve that completed pickup, so the policy returned to the first container instead of selecting the second. This breaks SUFFICIENCY. Add a count of completed pickups in the goal's requested order, initially 0. Increment once when the next required container has actually been lifted while grasped to approximately z=0.20, exposing its cube; do not increment on grasp closure or selection of a lift waypoint. Retain the count through lowering, release, and retreat. In the supplied two-pickup trajectories it becomes 1 at the observation of the first completed lift and remains 1 throughout the second approach, grasp, and commanded lift. In trajectories ending with the first lift command, it remains 0 because no completed lift has yet been observed. Example value: 1.",
      "value_type": "count"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "completed_pickups",
      "description": "After the first requested container was returned to the table, its cube was hidden again and the empty hand did not reveal that the pickup had already occurred. With only the initial scene and button flag, the policy repeated the first pickup, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Initialize completed_pickups to 0. Read the ordered container colors directly from the goal. Increment the incoming count once only when this cycle's motion frames show the container for the next uncompleted requested color being lifted with a closed grasp, reaching approximately z=0.20 and exposing the corresponding cube. Use the incoming count to identify which pickup qualifies; a continued hold or lowering of an already counted container must not increment it again. Do not increment merely because fingers closed or a lift is now commanded. Otherwise copy the incoming value, including through release and retreat. Example: write 1 upon observing the first successful lift, and retain 1 while approaching and grasping the second container."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Failures occurred in three related situations. At the button, the policy treated an approximately reached hover as the true button center and copied the measured TCP x,y into closing and pressing commands, retaining an approach error greater than 1 cm. At a target-container approach, it estimated a top center and yaw directly from the perspective image, producing the wrong world grasp pose. In ordered two-pickup goals, the unconditional instruction to keep holding the first container prevented release; after release the absence of progress handling caused repeated targeting of the first container or an incorrect retreat height. These are SUFFICIENCY failures in the action rules; pickup progress additionally requires the new completed_pickups memory.\n\nKeep the existing button-contact detection, press heights, and pre/post-press branching, but explicitly separate hover-arrival tolerance from target-coordinate estimation. Arrival tolerance only decides whether the next operation is closing or pressing. For every button hover, closing, pressing, and retraction command, estimate the circular button's actual world center from calibrated current imagery at its physical height. Never substitute the measured hover TCP x,y for that center. In particular, closing at hover commands the re-estimated center at z=0.15, and pressing commands that center at z=0.0075 with fingers closed. Use the existing nonidentical-waypoint safeguard for gripper-only changes.\n\nMake container pose reconstruction explicit. Associate containers with the requested cube locations in uncovered_scene using calibrated world geometry, not raw image centroid overlap. Reconstruct the visible square top's corners by intersecting calibrated camera rays with its top/grasp plane, approximately z=0.0633–0.0634. Compute the world-plane center and derive yaw from reconstructed world-plane edges, not image-plane slopes. Choose the equivalent square-aligned yaw closest to zero. Refine with current wrist geometry while accounting for the offset between optical center and TCP projection. Use this estimated container center for both its z=0.20 open-hand hover and its grasp-height descent with closure, rather than copying an offset realized hover position.\n\nReplace the unconditional instruction to keep holding every lifted container with ordered-goal handling. Read the requested color order from the goal; with an empty hand, completed_pickups selects the next uncompleted color. Current observed lift completion counts immediately for action selection, even though its updated memory is only carried into the next cycle. A closed grasp at approximately z=0.0634 still requires lifting to z=0.20 over the grasp center, retaining its square-aligned yaw and closed grip. Do not regard a commanded lift as completed.\n\nWhen a required container has visibly reached approximately z=0.20 in a closed grasp and another requested pickup remains, lower that held container at the current measured x,y to z=0.07, preserving the current orientation, and open on arrival. This returns the container over its original cube; retain pickup completion even when the cube is concealed again. This release rule takes priority over approaching the next target. With open fingers low over the returned container, retract vertically to approximately z=0.145, preserving current x,y and orientation and keeping the gripper open. This specific release-clearance rule replaces the generic z=0.20 fallback in that situation and takes priority over any grasp rule. Once the open hand has reached that clearance height, move directly to the next requested container's z=0.20 hover using completed_pickups and uncovered_scene. If already at that next container's hover, descend to its reconstructed grasp center and close; after grasping, lift it closed to z=0.20. Do not revisit the previously completed container or repeat the button press. When the last requested pickup is lifted, retain the closed grasp rather than returning or releasing it. Preserve the existing no-step recovery and nonidentical-waypoint safeguards; do not introduce purposeless waits."
}

