      "value_type": "count",
      "description": "The number of pickups completed in the goal's requested order. Initialize to 0. Increment once when the cycle shows the next required container actually lifted in a closed grasp to approximately z=0.20, exposing its cube. Do not increment on grasp closure or on selection of a lift waypoint. A continued hold or lowering of an already counted container does not count again. Retain the count through return, release, and retreat, even when the cube becomes concealed again. e.g. 1"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "completed_pickups",
      "description": "With empty memory initialize to 0. Read the requested color order from the goal. Increment the incoming count once when this cycle's motion frames show the container for the next uncompleted requested color being lifted in a closed grasp to approximately z=0.20 and exposing its cube. Use the incoming count to identify which pickup qualifies. Do not count continued holding or lowering of an already completed pickup, grasp closure alone, or a lift that is only being commanded. Otherwise copy the incoming value, including through return, release, and retreat. e.g. 1"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Apply the following rules to the observed situation. Use current contact evidence together with incoming button_pressed to recognize that the press has occurred; the outgoing memory is not a prerequisite for recognizing current contact.",
      "modify_to": "Apply the following rules to the observed situation. Use current contact evidence together with incoming button_pressed to recognize that the press has occurred; the outgoing memory is not a prerequisite for recognizing current contact. Likewise, use incoming completed_pickups together with any qualifying lift completion observed this cycle to determine the number of completed pickups for action selection. A commanded lift is not a completed pickup. Read the requested color order from the goal; with an empty hand, select the next uncompleted color. Never repeat a completed pickup or the completed button press.\n\nButton-hover arrival tolerance determines which operation is next, not the button's coordinates. For every button approach, closing, pressing, opening, and retraction command, reconstruct the circular button's actual world center from calibrated current imagery at its physical height. Never substitute the measured hover TCP x,y for that center. Closing at hover uses that center at z=0.15; pressing uses that center at z=0.0075. Preserve the nonidentical-waypoint safeguard for gripper-only changes."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "The target is the container covering the instructed colored cube in uncovered_scene. Estimate its current top grasp center and planar orientation from the visible square top and rim. Choose a downward orientation [3.1416, 0, yaw] aligned with the container's opposing grasp faces; use the equivalent square-aligned yaw closest to zero. Command [target_x, target_y, 0.20, 3.1416, 0, yaw] with the gripper open. When already above that container near z=0.20 with open fingers, descend to its top grasp center at z approximately 0.0634, with the same orientation, and close the gripper on arrival. Use wrist imagery to refine alignment, distinguishing camera optical center from TCP projection. Do not substitute the old colored cube's image centroid for the elevated container's current top center.",
      "modify_to": "The target is the container covering the next uncompleted requested color in uncovered_scene. Associate the remembered cube locations with current containers using calibrated world coordinates, not raw image-centroid overlap. Reconstruct the visible square top's corners by intersecting calibrated camera rays with its top/grasp plane, approximately z=0.0633–0.0634. Compute the world-plane center from these corners and derive yaw from the reconstructed world-plane edges, not image-plane slopes. Choose a downward orientation [3.1416, 0, yaw] aligned with opposing grasp faces, using the equivalent square-aligned yaw closest to zero. Refine the estimate with current wrist geometry, distinguishing the optical center from the TCP projection. Use this reconstructed container center for both the open-hand hover [target_x, target_y, 0.20, 3.1416, 0, yaw] and the descent to z approximately 0.0634 with closure on arrival. When already at the target hover with open fingers, descend rather than repeating the hover. Do not copy an offset realized hover position into the grasp command or substitute the remembered cube's image centroid for the elevated container's current top center. Apply the held-container return and release-clearance rules before any next-target approach or grasp."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "When the fingers have closed around the target at grasp height, typically leaving about 0.018 m on each finger, lift to z=0.20 over the same grasp center with the same yaw and gripper closed. Keep holding the container; do not release it or try to grasp the revealed cube.",
      "modify_to": "When the fingers have closed around the target at grasp height, typically leaving about 0.018 m on each finger, lift to z=0.20 over the same grasp center with the same yaw and gripper closed. Do not treat selection of this waypoint as completion.\n\nWhen the required container has actually reached approximately z=0.20 in a closed grasp and another requested pickup remains, return it over its original cube: command the current measured x,y at z=0.07, preserving the current orientation, and open on arrival. This return-and-release rule takes priority over approaching the next target. Preserve the completed pickup count even when the returned container conceals its cube.\n\nWith open fingers low over a returned, already completed container, retract vertically to approximately z=0.145 at the current x,y and orientation, keeping the gripper open. This release-clearance rule takes priority over grasp rules and the generic hover fallback. Once at this clearance height, move directly to the next uncompleted container's z=0.20 hover. Then descend, close, and lift according to the target rules. When the last requested pickup has actually been lifted, retain the closed grasp; do not return or release it. Never try to grasp a revealed cube."
    }
  ],
  "refused": ""
}
